name: sed
boss: Lookaround
flavor: I remember what you wrote. I match it again.
brief: sed edits a stream. It reads a line, applies your commands, prints the
brief: result. The one you will use forever is s/old/new/ -- and the trailing
brief: g, which changes every match on the line instead of only the first.
brief:
brief: -n turns off the automatic printing, which turns sed into a selector:
brief: -n '5p' prints only line 5. -i edits the file in place, and on a
brief: read-only root that only works on files you put somewhere writable.
---
q: replace the first @old@ on each line of @cfg@ with @new@
a: sed 's/@old@/@new@/' @cfg@
out: .
ref: sed
teach: sed
---
q: replace EVERY @old@ on every line of @cfg@ with @new@
a: sed 's/@old@/@new@/g' @cfg@
out: .
ref: sed
teach: sed
hint: the g goes after the final slash
---
q: replace @old@ with @new@ in @cfg@, ignoring case
a: sed 's/@old@/@new@/gi' @cfg@
a: sed 's/@old@/@new@/Ig' @cfg@
out: .
ref: sed
teach: sed
---
q: print only line 3 of @log@, and nothing else
a: sed -n '3p' @log@
out: WARN
ref: sed
teach: sed -n
hint: -n suppresses the automatic print, so only what you p yourself appears
---
q: print lines 2 through 4 of @log@
a: sed -n '2,4p' @log@
out: .
ref: sed
teach: sed -n
---
q: print every line of @log@ that mentions @sev@, using sed rather than grep
a: sed -n '/@sev@/p' @log@
out: @sev@
ref: sed
teach: sed -n
---
q: delete every line of @log@ mentioning @sev@
a: sed '/@sev@/d' @log@
out: .
ref: sed
teach: sed
---
q: delete the comment lines -- the ones starting with # -- from @cfg@
a: sed '/^#/d' @cfg@
out: listen
ref: sed
teach: sed
---
q: delete the blank lines from @cfg@
a: sed '/^$/d' @cfg@
out: timeout
ref: sed
teach: sed
---
q: delete the first line of @csv@, the header row
a: sed '1d' @csv@
out: ^gateway
ref: sed
teach: sed
---
q: strip every leading space from the lines of @log@
a: sed 's/^ *//' @log@
out: 2024
ref: sed
teach: sed
---
q: do two edits in one pass -- drop comments, then drop blank lines -- from @cfg@
a: sed -e '/^#/d' -e '/^$/d' @cfg@
out: listen
ref: sed
teach: sed -e
hint: -e lets you give more than one script
---
q: pull the number out of every port line in @cfg@ using an extended regex
a: sed -E 's/^port = ([0-9]+)$/\1/' @cfg@
out: 8080
ref: sed
teach: sed -E
hint: -E gives you (groups) without backslashes; \1 is what the group caught
---
q: same thing, but with the older flag letter for extended regular expressions
a: sed -r 's/^port = ([0-9]+)$/\1/' @cfg@
out: 8080
ref: sed
teach: sed -r
hint: -r and -E are the same flag; -E is the portable spelling
---
q: swap the first two comma-separated fields of @csv@
a: sed -E 's/^([^,]+),([^,]+)/\2,\1/' @csv@
out: .
ref: sed
teach: sed -E
---
q: copy @cfg@ to edit.ini and then change @old@ to @new@ inside it, in place
a: cp @cfg@ edit.ini && sed -i 's/@old@/@new@/g' edit.ini
state: exists:edit.ini
ref: sed
teach: sed -i
hint: -i writes the file back rather than printing it
---
q: print only the last line of @log@ using sed
a: sed -n '$p' @log@
out: out of memory
ref: sed
teach: sed -n
hint: $ means "the last line"
---
q: print every line of @log@ from the first @sev@ to the end
a: sed -n '/@sev@/,$p' @log@
out: .
ref: sed
teach: sed -n
---
q: run a sed script held in a file rather than typed on the command line
a: printf 's/@old@/@new@/g\n' > s.sed && sed -f s.sed @cfg@
out: .
ref: sed
teach: sed -f
---
q: edit a copy in place, keeping the original as edit.ini.bak
a: cp @cfg@ edit.ini && sed -i.bak 's/@old@/@new@/g' edit.ini
state: exists:edit.ini.bak
ref: sed
teach: sed -i[SFX]
hint: a suffix stuck to -i means "back the original up under this name"
